assistant_message_1=output_text I’ll inspect the exact prompt and translator first, then design the smallest semantics needed for the translated program and verify both concrete execution and symbolic claims. I’ll keep the proof commands reproducible in `prove.sh`.
assistant_message_2=output_text The target is integer GCD. I’m using an iterative Euclidean implementation with explicit sign normalization, so it handles negative inputs and `(0, 0)` consistently as well as the prompt examples. The proof will separate a reusable loop theorem from whole-program correctness: first prove the loop theorem against the operational semantics, then use that proved theorem in the verification layer.
assistant_message_3=output_text The parser and semantics compile cleanly. Concrete execution is currently hanging, so I’m tracing the transition rules before building proofs; this is likely a control-cell sequencing issue rather than the Euclidean algorithm itself.
assistant_message_4=output_text Concrete runs now terminate with the expected results, including negative and zero cases. The remaining issue is proof induction: the prover is unrolling symbolic loop branches instead of applying the loop claim circularly, so I’m checking this K release’s reachability-claim mechanism and adjusting the invariant encoding.
assistant_message_5=output_text The loop invariant is now closing by circularity. The subtle point was the temporary `r`: it is arbitrary if the loop executes zero times, but equals the new `b` at recursive loop entries. Encoding that distinction made the induction valid rather than over-constraining the base case.
assistant_message_6=output_text Both positive proofs now close: the loop theorem prints `#Top`, and the universal whole-program claim prints `#Top`. I’m packaging the exact staged commands and doing one clean end-to-end rerun, including `krun` checks for both prompt examples and signed/zero edge cases.
